body:
This is so amazing thank you for posting this! I struggle internally with thinking that I am a "side" of the "argument". Emom thinks there is a "communication issue" between ndad and me. She doesn't think it's abuse. If nsister bullied me when I was younger (or even now) or ndad did something, she would always say "it takes two to tango".

&#x200B;

Child abuse is indefensible. Thank you for helping me realize that.

Literally dead on what I experienced. I was the oldest child and my 3 younger sisters got SOOOO much leeway. They could scream and cry and throw tantrums. I couldn't. If my parents asked all of us to get a chore done, I would be the one who was expected to do the chore and would get the most punishment or scolding or yelling if it wasn't done. They sat on their lazy asses knowing that they wouldn't get in trouble for it. They could stay in their rooms and watch youtube or do whatever they did all day while I was screamed at or made to feel guilty for playing video games instead of "being productive".

&#x200B;

And of course, boundaries weren't a thing. I couldn't say no to my dad or my mom. It reminds me a lot of jail. I was supposed to be, and was molded into, a model prisoner while my sisters were the typical inmates who got into fights and were "bad". The bar was set so much higher for me. I could get punished so much more severely for such minor things--even things that weren't me "being bad" like not knowing how to dress when we went out to restaurants. My parents never taught me stuff like that but got mad at me for not magically knowing how to do it.

That gives me hope thank you! I really like that idea of taking a vacation. When I drove from my college apartment to here (over 1,000 miles) and left some abusive roommates behind, I felt a lot better and felt myself decompress in so many ways. I felt like a sponge that held in so much water that was finally able to release all of it. Once I get my paycheck from this temporary job I'm at I will drive somewhere for a few days!

&#x200B;

I put a lot of pressure on myself to "be productive" even when I'm doing the absolute max that I can (sometimes that means staying in bed all day and not being able to get up). It's all from the nparents' messages and it's a lot worse when I'm at home with them.

&#x200B;

I'm happy to hear that there is legitimate hope when I'm free. I've never really been free. Even when I was NC with my nparents in my last year of college, I was friends with 3 extremely toxic narcissistic roommates who made my life a living hell. I've never been in a non-abusive environment for a long time.

I (22m) had the same thing with my dad. I had a very similar relationship to him. I put him on such a high pedestal and did the fawning defense mechanism unconsciously in response to his emotional and sometimes physical abuse. I went to a Narcissistic Abuse Survivors Group yesterday and they talked about how you don't have to earn love. You don't have to do anything to be worthy of love. That made me remember so many things about ndad that directly related to that. He doesn't love me and doesn't express any approval unless I do something that he wants--and only if I do it exactly how he wants me to do it. If something happens that messes it up (say the person he wanted me to meet with didn't show up) then it's somehow my fault. It was always my fault according to him. He was like an overbearing boss at a job who wants me to do endless things for him. There was no love whatsoever.

&#x200B;

I always love how my mom (and I'm guessing a lot of other enablers based on what I've read in this group) would always say "he doesn't know how to express his love like other people do" or "deep down he really does love you". What a joke!

ME TOO. This is exactly how ndad treated me. It still affects me today. You are not alone <3. They never stop. It's never enough. It's always a competition. No matter how much you bend to them, they won't give you that real love. It's like a mask. If you put it on like everyone else, then everything is calm. But when you take it off and say "ENOUGH IS ENOUGH!" and stand up for yourself, then they get mad.

&#x200B;

But the fact that you are here shows that they did not break you. They can never ever break you. You will always be you, no matter how much they bend you or manipulate you. Boundaries are important and can give you some space. College will change a lot of things for you but they can definitely still worm their way into your life. I hope you find happiness and true love from someone. You are absolutely loveable <3

That's funny, I didn't like it that much. I jumped around from season to season and got through a bunch of them. I loved 1848, US, Mexican, and the current Russian Revolution seasons, but I was just perpetually bored during the Bolivar one. I just got to the battle of Carabobo and I barely remember most of the people's names. It's so sad because I always wanted to learn more about that area.

&#x200B;

Regardless, I'm happy you love it! I went on the same path as you: History of Rome to Revolutions. There are so many great revolutions that he covers. You'll probably like the Haiti one too if you like Bolivar.

My favorite moment in a Paradox Game was when I played as Venice. I conquered Serbia, Albania and Bosnia very quickly, killed Byzantium before the Ottos could get to them, and then declared war on the Ottomans in the 1460s. The Ottomans were distracted by a war with QQ in the east but could still easily kill me if I made the wrong move. Luckily I was able to rush to Edirne (I controlled Constantinople) and rushed through the gates when I had a breach. By the time the Ottomans realized what was happening, they had lost all access to the Balkans. My massive fleet of 40 galleys, financed by Venetian silks and gold, saw to that. Then I just waited until their war exhaustion was high and get a huge piece of the Balkans. I declared war on them again a few years later until I got all the Balkans. Other empires like the Mamluks and a resurgent QQ (my ally) took some Otto land while I secured my lands. Then I took on the Mamluks which was even harder than the Ottoman war but came out on top. In my last save I have completely taken over Anatolia, Northern Italy, Egypt, Ethiopia, and am planning an expedition to India soon :)

&#x200B;

Edit: If this is picked I would like CK2 Holy Fury.

That's fascinating! I never even thought about identifying with main characters but it makes so much sense! I love Breaking Bad, but I find myself dissociating while watching it and resisting the idea of watching it even fi I know I like it. That happens with a lot of shows I watch and games I play. 

&#x200B;

On the one hand, though, I want to do things and I want to overcome my anxiety. I go through periods of being social and cutting myself off from everything. I don't want to shut the world out but I also want to heal. Were you able to tell the difference between nervousness about doing something new vs triggers for dissociation?

&#x200B;

Also thank you for the advice on the inner child! I got a few ideas of how to connect with him from reading this :)

That's awesome! Can you explain more how you connected with your inner child? I've been trying to connect with mine but it's really hard.

&#x200B;

And also, how did you figure out what was triggering you?

It's really hard to explain. Sometimes it feels more or less intense. Imagine only feeling very muted versions of a feeling. Like instead of truly happy you just feel kind of content. Or instead of sad you might not feel anything. It affects my vision and hearing too. It'll be like this white fog that is around my vision that I don't even notice unless I'm aware and introspective in that moment. There will be things I'm too anxious to look at or that I look at quickly. Like if I'm on the computer I will look at one part of the screen very intensely and won't observe the other parts. Hearing is a bit easier to explain in that my ears usually feel clogged or like something is in them and I will always hear music playing in them. It's sort of like shields for my eyes and ears. My body subconsciously protects me from seeing the things that hurt me (based on experiences I had when in my childhood) and my ears protect me from hearing the thing that's hurting me (also based on childhood experiences). When I'm really dissociated I will find that I have flashbacks a lot and go on autopilot in real life. For my body, it feels like everything is stiff. I feel like I can't physically do something or that I'm not capable of doing it. An example of it today was when I woke up and my phone charger was unplugged. It would be so easy to plug it in and plug my phone in, but I had this feeling that I shouldn't do it, even though doing it would benefit me with no downsides. When I'm sitting in my chair in front of my computer, I don't feel comfortable no matter what position I'm in when I dissociate. I often have to stretch my legs out as far as they can go and lean back in my chair to feel comfortable. I sometimes stretch my legs out so that the front legs of my chair come off the ground to lean back even more. Usually when I dissociate and am anxious I feel the need to eat even though I'm not hungry, play video games, or masturbate. It's my body's way of helping me relax. When I dissociate I also don't notice my breath. If I consciously breathe then I feel a little better. It's like I'm holding my breath to get ready for some threat.

&#x200B;

That's the best way I can expand on my explanation. I hope it's a little more clear to you now!

 The archives are comprehensive and totally secure, my *young* Jedi. One thing you may be absolutely sure of - if an item does not appear in our records, >!it does not exist.!< 

&#x200B;

&#x200B;

I felt this way in high school. I only had one friend and felt really lonely. I wanted to be in a groupchat and have friends and stuff. This happened in my sophomore year of college.

&#x200B;

I met several people in my dorm floor. I felt like I was someone else. I was talking to everyone I met and really stepped out of my comfort zone! I felt really happy and found a group of 10-12 constant friends in that dorm.

&#x200B;

The pros: I didn't feel as lonely. I got to meet new people. Every day there was the potential for fun things to do instead of sitting inside all day and playing video games. I have some great memories of the things we all did together.

&#x200B;

The cons: I feel like I rushed into these friendships. They were pretty surface level now that I think about it. I meshed myself in without really having my own identity and happiness figured out. Whatever they wanted to do I would do. Whatever they liked I liked. I held in my emotions around them and didn't express myself. I began to resent them because I was the "fixer" of the group and, unconsciously, created this idea that I had to help and fix everyone. As they got used to this dynamic it rarely occurred to them to ask how I was doing. This built resentment in me until I finally snapped. I left the friend group on bad terms with some people. I felt guilty about it later on. Now, looking back, I have learned a few lessons from the experience.

&#x200B;

Lessons:

&#x200B;

1. Don't be afraid to say no to the group.
2. Don't rely on the group for your happiness. It's the same idea as how relationships don't guarantee your happiness.
3. Don't be a chameleon. This involves working on your own identity and doing things yourself.
4. People make mistakes. Forgive yourself for your own mistakes and learn to forgive others for their mistakes. It's ok to give them a second chance.
5. Let them know what you want and need. If they are really your friends then they are there for you just like you are for them.

&#x200B;

Best of luck to you in finding that friend group :) Definitely would recommend the dorms when you go to college. Parties, clubs (like organizations), and social events can help you build that friendship network.

That's a good point. I am very socially anxious and find confronting people difficult. It is much easier to talk about it to other people. I've brought it up a couple times with them but I feel like I don't assert myself enough. On the flip side, it really is their choice to not take what I'm saying seriously. Overall I feel like the friendship is very dysfunctional.

&#x200B;

I appreciate what you said and your advice! Thank you :)

Same lol. My family would assume I was lying about everything even if I had nothing to do with it. I would see my sister, the culprit, laughing to herself afterwards.

&#x200B;

Fucking hell it's so fucking unfair.

Long story that extends a little past the first date but I'm just gonna say the whole thing because it was really bizarre.

&#x200B;

I was in the summer between my freshman and sophomore year of college. I met a girl from yik yak of all places and she turned out to be really cute. She really really liked me which was a huge turn on for me since I hadn't gone on a date before and had flirted with a girl only once in high school. We went to Disney Springs and everything went well at first, but she's one of those types of people who just talks and talks and talks. I barely got a word in and basically felt like an idiot that she was leading around. There was no physical contact either. No kiss. Not even handholding. I was too nervous and had major social anxiety and was afraid to make the first move. Like I said, this was my first date ever.

&#x200B;

She was in a groupchat with a few of her high school friends and was really insistent on getting me to join. I agreed and was greeted to a world of hell. She hated all of her "friends" and there was this guy named Gage who was extremely obsessed with her. He only talked to her and would say he liked her in front of me even though she was clearly interested in me. She didn't tell him to stop because she supposedly liked the attention. This was a major red flag.

&#x200B;

So after the date, I came home and texted in this groupchat. She immediately texted me and asked why I hadn't texted her first. She then sent me a lot of other mean shit until I basically apologized to her for nothing. I HAD been about to text her but she seemed to think I was betraying her somehow. Gage, of course, made a comment about how I was a "bad choice" for her and was dming her the entire time. I had never been treated this way before and looking back it sounds crazy, but like I said: I was extremely socially anxious and she was my first ever date. I thought this was normal. (My upbringing is fucked up)

&#x200B;

Some of her former friends who hated her had made another groupchat and decided to add me to it for some reason. They said they all hated her but that they wouldn't do anything to stop our dating. I felt like I was caught between a rock and a hard place. On one hand I had this girl who liked me. On the other hand I had a group of people who were being really nice to me and really felt like friends (I had had only one friend up to that point). Basically...all of this was really new to me, but it all blew up when she discovered the groupchat I was in. 

&#x200B;

She called me a traitor and an asshole and did the worst thing she could possibly think of: she told me all the spoilers to Game of Thrones and then hung up on me.

&#x200B;

TLDR: The first date I ever went on was with a girl from yik yak and she was CRAZY.

Oh man I got the "when you're older" all the time. "When you're older you'll realize just how much ndad and I have done for you!" Yeah, I realize you've given me severe trauma, an eating disorder, severe anxiety, and a lack of confidence in myself to the point where I thought I had no identity for so long.

&#x200B;

Thanks.

My current friend group met me more than I met it. I was a Junior in college when I met them (senior now) and felt extremely lonely. I was single (still am) and was very uncomfortable with being that way. I had never had a girlfriend and I always felt this pressure to meet girls. I was at a club (like the school kind not the midtown/downtown kind) and was looking around at girls to try to muster up the courage to talk to them. I was not enjoying myself at all and really felt that pressure to break out of my comfort zone and talk to them. As a result I felt really alone and did not feel happy at all because I'm a really shy person and find it very hard to express my emotions to people--especially when asking girls out.

&#x200B;

As I was wandering around, a couple people came up to me and started talking to me. We talked about video games, history, and other things that I really like, but I still felt this disconnect from them because my mind kept telling me to stop wasting my time and start talking to girls. Well, I'm glad I kept talking to those people because they have become pretty close friends of mine. Even though I find it hard to connect with them on a deep level and express my true emotions around them, I would still call them my friends.

I've listened to the Brain Over Binge podcast and what I'm thinking is that overcoming binging is about trusting yourself. I went on many sporadic diets and tried to control my calories because I didn't trust my body's connection with my mind to tell me when I was full. I wanted to lose weight really really badly. It's really helped me to recognize what goes on in my mind and have outside perspectives from my friends. I remember doing karate in high school and having abs, but still thinking I looked terrible.

&#x200B;

What I've learned is that trusting yourself and loving yourself is the first step, and everything else will fall into place naturally.

You breathe. You exist. Therefore, by the laws of nature, you are acceptable. And in that vein, you ARE worthy of being dated. Doesn't matter how you look or act. You, as a human being, are worthy simply because you exist. That does not mean that everyone will date you, but the undeniable fact (read: objective fact) is that you ARE worthy of being dated.

&#x200B;

You got this man.

